}{y-69} = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find a common denominator for the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45)}{(y-29)(y-45)(y-69)} = 0.\n\\]\nSince the denominator cannot be zero, we focus on the numerator:\n\\[\n(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45).\n\\]\n\nWe expand each term separately:\n\\[\n(y-45)(y-69) = y^2 - 69y - 45y + 3105 = y^2 - 114y + 3105,\n\\]\n\\[\n(y-29)(y-69) = y^2 - 69y - 29y + 2001 = y^2 - 98y + 2001,\n\\]\n\\[\n2(y-29)(y-45) = 2(y^2 - 45y - 29y + 1305) = 2(y^2 - 74y + 1305) = 2y^2 - 148y + 2610.\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute these expanded forms back into the numerator:\n\\[\n(y^2 - 114y + 3105) + (y^2 - 98y + 2001) - (2y^2 - 148y + 2610).\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\ny^2 - 114y + 3105 + y^2 - 98y + 2001 - 2y^2 + 148y - 2610.\n\\]\n\\[\n= (y^2 + y^2 - 2y^2) + (-114y - 98y + 148y) + (3105 + 2001 - 2610).\n\\]\n\\[\n= 0y^2 - 64y + 2496.\n\\]\n\nThe equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n-64y + 2496 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-64y = -2496,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2496}{64} = 39.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\( y = x^2 - 10x \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x = 39.\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\):\n\\[\na = 1, \\quad b = -10, \\quad c = -39.\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-(-10) \\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-39)}}{2 \\cdot 1},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 + 16}{2} = 13,\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 - 16}{2} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the equation \n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x^2-10x-29} + \\frac{1}{x^2-10x-45} - \\frac{2}{x^2-10x-69} = 0,\n\\]\nwe will start by introducing a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \n\\[\ny = x^2 - 10x.\n\\]\nThen the equation becomes\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{y-29} + \\frac{1}{y-45} - \\frac{2}{y-69} = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find a common denominator for the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45)}{(y-29)(y-45)(y-69)} = 0.\n\\]\nSince the denominator cannot be zero, we focus on the numerator:\n\\[\n(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45).\n\\]\n\nWe expand each term separately:\n\\[\n(y-45)(y-69) = y^2 - 69y - 45y + 3105 = y^2 - 114y + 3105,\n\\]\n\\[\n(y-29)(y-69) = y^2 - 69y - 29y + 2001 = y^2 - 98y + 2001,\n\\]\n\\[\n2(y-29)(y-45) = 2(y^2 - 45y - 29y + 1305) = 2(y^2 - 74y + 1305) = 2y^2 - 148y + 2610.\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute these expanded forms back into the numerator:\n\\[\n(y^2 - 114y + 3105) + (y^2 - 98y + 2001) - (2y^2 - 148y + 2610).\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\ny^2 - 114y + 3105 + y^2 - 98y + 2001 - 2y^2 + 148y - 2610.\n\\]\n\\[\n= (y^2 + y^2 - 2y^2) + (-114y - 98y + 148y) + (3105 + 2001 - 2610).\n\\]\n\\[\n= 0y^2 - 64y + 2496.\n\\]\n\nThe equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n-64y + 2496 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-64y = -2496,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2496}{64} = 39.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\( y = x^2 - 10x \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x = 39.\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\):\n\\[\na = 1, \\quad b = -10, \\quad c = -39.\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-(-10) \\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-39)}}{2 \\cdot 1},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 + 16}{2} = 13,\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 - 16}{2} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:571"}
